 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs in order (`t1, t2, …`) and resets `next` to follow the last one, so
+  the URL never has gaps (`?tabs=t1,t2`, not `t1,t3`).
+- Reason: maintainer decision — gapped keys in the URL look sloppy.
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys (2026-09-02), which forbade renumbering because the key is also the React key: shifting
+  keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident
+  2026-08-29). That cost is accepted unless the React key is decoupled from the URL key.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "open after close continues contiguously")
